{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If in My statutes you walk, etc.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said, may their memory be for blessing: perhaps this refers to the commandments? But when it says ״וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי״ etc., behold the commandments are already stated — so what do I fulfill by ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי״? To be toiling in Torah. This far. And the reason the verse calls the toil of Torah a חֻקָּה is because there is a commandment even to review things one has learned two and three times, which are already planted within him — for HaShem desires the engagement of Torah; He enacted it as a חֻקָּה. And you will find that they said, may their memory be for blessing (Ecclesiastes Rabbah 3), that the reason a person should study Torah with constant passion is because the wisdom of the Blessed One decreed that a person will study and forget." "And the reason it says <b>חֻקֹּתַי</b> in the plural is to allude to two Torot — the Written Torah and the Oral Torah — while the form of the word is singular, for there is no ו between the ק and the ת, to say that the Oral Torah is included within the Written Torah, and there is their structure. Further, it alludes by saying <b>חֻקֹּתַי</b> in the plural, in the manner of His saying (Joshua 1:8): ״וְהָגִיתָ בּוֹ יוֹמָם וָלַיְלָה״ — that one must set fixed times for Torah by day and by night, and the form is singular because the two times are in one day. It further intends that a person needs two things in the fulfillment of the commandments of the Torah: one, to learn for himself, and the second, to teach others, as it is written (Deuteronomy 11:19): ״וְלִמַּדְתֶּם אֹתָם אֶת בְּנֵיכֶם וְשִׁנַּנְתָּ״ etc. Therefore it says <b>חֻקֹּתַי</b> in the plural — to learn and to teach, to observe and to do, as He said: ״וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״." "<b>And the reason</b> it uses the language of walking is to say that one must engage in Torah even while walking, in the manner of what they said (Deuteronomy 6:7): ״וּבְלֶכְתְּךָ בַדֶּרֶךְ״ — through love of it he will err constantly, etc.:" "It further intends, in the manner of what they said, may their memory be for blessing (Leviticus Rabbah 35:), on the verse ״חִשַּׁבְתִּי דְרָכַי״ etc., that David would intend to do other matters, but his feet would carry him of themselves to the house of study — from the abundance of desire and will and habituation in the matter. And this is what it says: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b> — meaning, your walking of yourselves will be after it, as David said, from the abundance of passion and habituation." "It further intends, according to their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Zohar III:200a), that the Torah has four ways, and they are: peshat, remez, derash, and sod, and from these seventy faces branch off, and each mode into many paths and trails and byways — and this is what it says: <b>חֻקֹּתַי</b> in the plural, <b>תֵּלֵכוּ</b> — meaning, in every path and trail he shall walk through the explanations of the verses, and not say that the Torah has nothing but the peshat that is understood by all." "Further, it may intend by saying <b>תֵּלֵכוּ</b> ('you shall walk') along the lines of the verse (Proverbs 3:6): ״בְּכָל דְּרָכֶיךָ דָעֵהוּ״ ('In all your ways know Him') etc. And Rambam wrote in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Yesodei HaTorah (Hilkhot De'ot), and this is his language: he should set his heart so that his body be whole and strong, so that it shall be etc. to know HaShem, for it is impossible that he understand and contemplate the wisdoms etc. — thus one who walks in this path all his days is continually serving HaShem — end quote. And this is the meaning of the verse here: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b> ('If in My statutes you shall walk') — for the sake of engagement in Torah you shall walk in all your ways, in eating etc., in drinking, in speech etc., in every act that arises from natural circumstances." "Further, it may be explained along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for blessing (Sanhedrin 34a), that permission is granted to those who learn Torah to interpret it and to expound it through many paths and trails, and a seasoned student may innovate in the exposition of verses as far as the verse can bear, according to all that his hand reaches in his Torah study. And HaShem commands here by saying <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> — which is the Torah — <b>תֵּלֵכוּ</b> ('you shall walk') in its pardes [mystical orchard]. This is a condition: ״וְאֶת מִצְוֺתַי״ ('and My commandments') etc. — meaning, that one not show a face in the Torah contrary to the law, so as not to declare the impure pure, ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ ('and you shall do them') — so as not to declare the pure impure. And this is their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Avot 3:11): one who shows a face in the Torah contrary to the law has no share in the World to Come." "Further, it may be explained along the lines of their statement (Avot 2:6): ״וְלֹא עַם הָאָרֶץ חָסִיד״ ('and an ignoramus cannot be a pietist') — meaning, that it is forbidden for an ignoramus to conduct himself with piety, making stringencies and fences according to the practice of the pietists, for sometimes he will make a stringency in a matter that is on the contrary a transgression. For one may find a pietist who wishes to fence himself to fulfill the conjugal obligation on the consecrated days, and it will seem in his eyes a great mitzvah to lie with his wife on the night of Yom Kippur — as we have heard that such a thing once occurred — and the like. Therefore the Sages, may their memory be for blessing, commanded that an ignoramus must not conduct himself with the trait of piety. And this is the meaning of HaShem's statement here: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b> ('If in My statutes you shall walk') — which is engagement in Torah — then <b>וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b> ('and My commandments you shall keep') — meaning, you shall make a safeguard for yourselves in order to fulfill them: fences and protections, and not otherwise [i.e., only one versed in Torah may properly make fences]." "Further, it may intend along the lines of their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Avot 4:1): ״הֱוֵי גּוֹלֶה לִמְקוֹם תּוֹרָה״ ('Exile yourself to a place of Torah') — that one needs to set his feet journeying from place to place to learn Torah. Go out and learn from their words, may their memory be for blessing, in the Gemara (Chagigah 5b): for all of them went into exile to learn, and there was one who would travel six months and return likewise, to engage in study for a single day. And this is the meaning of <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> — which is engagement in Torah: you must go from your place after it. Also, in order that you be free [to study], it is not proper to learn while in his own house, for the needs of the household will distract him; therefore let a man leave his father and his mother and journey from his place to walk after it." "<b>And His saying</b> ״וְאֶת מִצְוֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ״ ('and My commandments you shall keep') may be explained along the lines of what they said in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 31a): Rabbi Yannai proclaimed: ״חֲבָל עַל מָאן דְּלֵית לֵיהּ דַּרְתָּא, וְתַרְעָא לְדַרְתָּא עָבִיד״ ('Woe to him who has no courtyard yet makes a gate for the courtyard') — that the essence of engagement in Torah must be in order to keep and to do. And this is the meaning of <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> etc., which is engagement in Torah — this is a condition: ״וְאֶת מִצְוֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ ('and My commandments you shall keep and do them'). And His saying <b>וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי</b> may be explained along the lines of their statement in the Gemara (Berakhot 17a): one who learns not for its own sake — it would have been better for him had he not been created. And the Tosafot raised a difficulty against this, in light of that which they said: a person should always engage in Torah even if not for its own sake, for from [learning] not for its own sake one comes to [learn] for its own sake. And they resolved that there are two modes of not for its own sake: one is to contend, and one is to be honored. To contend — it would have been better for him not to have been created; to be honored — he should engage, for from etc. And this is the meaning of HaShem's statement: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b> — and this is a condition: ״וְאֶת מִצְוֺתַי״ — that in it [the Torah] there are two commandments required in its learning: that they not learn either to contend or to be honored. Then <b>תִּשְׁמְרוּ וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם</b> ('you shall keep and do them'). The verse promises that even if a transgression comes to his hand he will be saved from it, as they said (Sotah 21a): Torah protects and saves from the evil inclination, and as they said in tractate Avodah Zarah (Avodah Zarah 17a) regarding that incident of Rabbi Chanina and Rabbi Yonatan who passed by the door of prostitutes etc., as it is written (Proverbs 2:11): ״מְזִמָּה תִּשְׁמֹר עָלֶיךָ״ ('Discretion will guard you') etc. And He was precise to say <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם</b> ('and you shall do') along the lines of their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Kiddushin 39b): if a person sat and did not transgress, he is given reward like one who performs a mitzvah — and they established this regarding a case where a transgression came to his hand and he was saved from it. Even though it is nothing but a keeping [i.e., refraining], the verse reckons it upon him as though he performed the act of a mitzvah." "<b>And even though</b> Rava expounded (Pesachim 50a) on the verse ״כִּי גָדוֹל עַד שָׁמַיִם חַסְדֶּךָ״ (Psalms 57:11) — this refers to one who studies not for its own sake but for honor — the reason is not that there is desire on the part of the Creator for such study, but rather for the outcome that emerges from it, that he will come to study for its own sake. And such Torah does not have within its inherent power to save one from the evil inclination when a transgression comes to hand. And with this one may resolve what they asked there in Sotah according to Rav Yosef, who said that Torah — whether at a time when one is engaged in it or at a time when one is not engaged in it — protects and saves; if so, Doeg and Ahithophel — did they not engage in Torah? Why did it not protect them? End of quotation. According to our words there is no difficulty, for they were studying from the outset not for its own sake, and such Torah has no power to defend a person from the evil inclination." "Or it may say in this manner: since there are two positions (Avodah Zarah 17a), one of Rabbi Ḥanina and one of Rabbi Yonatan — one said ״נִחְלַף אַפִּתְחָא דַּעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה דְּנָכִיס יִצְרֵיהּ״ ('let him go to the entrance of idolatry, and perhaps his inclination will be placated'), and one said ״נִחְלַף אַפִּתְחָא דְּזוֹנוֹת כִּי הֵיכִי דְּנִכְפְּיֵיהּ לְיִצְרִין וּנְקַבֵּל אַגְרָא״ ('let him go to the entrance of prostitutes, so that he may subdue his inclination and receive reward') — the verse comes here and rules in accordance with the one who says נִחְלַף אַפִּתְחָא דְּזוֹנוֹת. And that is His saying <b>if in My statutes you walk</b> — meaning, you are able to go [to the entrance of prostitutes] and I assure you that ״אֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ״, that they will subdue their inclination. And if you say: why should he bring himself into such doubt? — for this He said <b>and you shall do them</b>, so that you may receive the reward of one who performs a mitzvah, since the sin has come to your hand. And that is their statement there: ״וְנִכְפְּיֵיהּ לְיִצְרִין כִּי הֵיכִי דִּנְקַבֵּל אַגְרָא״ ('and he subdues his inclination so that he may receive reward')." "Or it further intends according to their statement, of blessed memory, in the chapter of the pious (Avot, ch. 6): the Torah is acquired through forty-eight things. For not everyone who wishes to acquire the Torah will attain its acquisition except through the intermediary of those forty-eight things. And that is what He says here: <b>if in My statutes you walk</b> — if you desire to attain the Torah, this is a precondition: ״וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אוֹתָם״ — for these are those forty-eight things, since among them there is the aspect of restraints, and corresponding to them He said ״תִּשְׁמְרוּ״ ('you shall keep'), and among them there is the aspect of action, and corresponding to them He said ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם״ ('and you shall do'), etc." "It further intends to inform us of the surpassing virtue of engagement in Torah, which possesses something that engagement in mitzvot does not. For in the case of engagement in mitzvot, it is possible that a person may be active and do them, and then the sin may cause [their undoing] and exchange his honor for disgrace, and ״לֹא תִזָּכַרְנָה צִדְקָתוֹ בְּיוֹם פִּשְׁעוֹ״ ('his righteousness will not be remembered on the day of his transgression') (Ezekiel 18:24). But the mitzvah of engagement in Torah assures him that for all who engage in Torah, his righteousness stands forever and his merit goes with him, according to their statement (Sotah 21a) that a transfection extinguishes a mitzvah, but no transgression extinguishes Torah so as to nullify the merit of Torah. And he will merit to walk in it [the Torah] as he goes to the supernal world. And see what I explained in parashat Acharei Mot (Leviticus 18:4): ״אֶת חֻקֹּתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ לָלֶכֶת בָּהֶם״." "It further intends according to their statement, of blessed memory (Shabbat 63a), on the verse ״אֹרֶךְ יָמִים בִּימִינָהּ״ etc. (Proverbs 3:16), that those who make it their right hand — their reward is in the supernal world, and those who make it their left hand — their reward is in this world. That is what He says here: <b>if in My statutes</b> — which is engagement in Torah — your intention in this engagement should not be for the purpose of this world, which is the category of those who make it their left hand; rather, ״תֵּלֵכוּ״ ('you shall walk') — your intention should be for the payment when you go to the supernal world, and that is the category of those who make it their right hand." "It may further be explained according to the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory, in Mishnat Chasidim (Avot 3:9): ״כָּל שֶׁחָכְמָתוֹ מְרֻבָּה מִמַּעֲשָׂיו״ etc., and ״כָּל שֶׁמַּעֲשָׂיו מְרֻבִּים״ etc. — and this is what the verse alludes to in saying <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b>, which is the engagement of Torah, and corresponding to this measure will be the observance of the commandments and their fulfillment, so that his wisdom not exceed his deeds. And this is what it says adjacent to the matter of engaging in Torah: ״וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״." "It may further be explained according to their statement, of blessed memory (Pe'ah 1:1): ״אֵלּוּ דְבָרִים״ etc. ״וְתַלְמוּד תּוֹרָה כְּנֶגֶד כֻּלָּם״ — and this is what it says: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b>, which is the engagement of Torah — I account it for you as though you have also kept My commandments and done them." "Or it may intend according to what it says (Leviticus 7:37): ״זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה לָעוֹלָה וְלַמִּנְחָה״, and the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded (Menachot 110a): one who engages in the passage of the burnt-offering, it is accounted for him as though he offered a burnt-offering, etc. — according to what it says (Hosea 14:3–4): ״וּנְשַׁלְּמָה פָרִים שְׂפָתֵינוּ״. And not this alone, but every single commandment that is not beyond one's reach — when a person studies its law in the Torah, it is as though he performed it. And this is the verse's statement: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי</b> etc. — those that have no possibility of being performed, <b>תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b> — meaning, you will anticipate them: when will they come into your hands to perform them? I account it for you as though you have also performed them. And this is what it says <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אוֹתָם</b> — to say: I am not giving reward for thought alone. And although we wrote just above ״וְתַלְמוּד תּוֹרָה כְּנֶגֶד כֻּלָּם״, there are commandments that override Torah study for their sake, such as the act of offerings and the like; and for this consideration one must study their law and look forward to performing them." "Or it may say along this line: that a person should not say, since Torah study fills the place of the commandments, if so he is exempt from performing the commandments that present themselves to him — therefore Scripture says <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b>, which is the engagement of Torah, <b>וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי</b> — which are negative commandments — <b>תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b>: not for this reason are you exempt from fulfilling commandments, but rather ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״. And what I explained as it being equivalent — that is a specification for one that presents itself and cannot be fulfilled through others." "It may further intend according to their statement, of blessed memory (Berachot 14a), that it is forbidden for a person to set his feet on the road until he first puts words of Torah, as it is written (Psalms 85:14): ״צֶדֶק לְפָנָיו יְהַלֵּךְ וְיָשֵׂם לְדֶרֶךְ״ etc. — and go out and learn what they said in the Zohar (1:69a) concerning those who walk on the road without words of Torah among them, that they incur mortal danger. And this is what HaShem says: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b> — meaning, when there is Torah study, then you may walk in safety, for when there is engagement of Torah its forces escort the person." "It further intends to say that which they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 63a): if a person intended to perform a mitzvah and was prevented and did not do it, Scripture credits him as though he did it — and this is what the verse means: <b>if in My statutes you shall walk</b> — in the future tense, meaning, you will intend to walk, etc.; <b>you shall keep</b> — likewise, you will intend to observe; <b>and you shall do them</b> — meaning, I credit you as though you have already done them." "It further intends, after the manner of what they said, of blessed memory (Sukkah 45b), that there are three levels among the men of ascent: the first — those who gaze through a speculum that does not shine; the second — those who gaze through a speculum that shines; the third — those who fly [עַיְּלֵי] without a bar. And I have explained along these lines the verse (Job 3:19): ״קָטֹן וְגָדוֹל שָׁם הוּא וְעֶבֶד חָפְשִׁי מֵאֲדֹנָיו״ — קָטֹן (small) is the one who gazes through a speculum that does not shine, in the sod of (Genesis 1:16) the lesser luminary (הַמָּאוֹר הַקָּטֹן); גָּדוֹל (great) is the one who gazes through a speculum that shines, in the sod of (ibid.) the greater luminary (הַמָּאוֹר הַגָּדוֹל); and עֶבֶד חָפְשִׁי מֵאֲדֹנָיו (a slave freed of his master) — this is one who ascends without permission, for HaShem, who is his master, has given him freedom to go wherever he desires. And I do not know by what means this great distinction can be grasped. For this reason His blessed statement came and said <b>if in My statutes</b> — meaning, through the striving of Torah, as needed in this matter, you do not need permission to conduct yourselves [upward]; rather, you yourselves shall walk, without a bar." "It further intends, after the manner of what they said, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 102a), that HaShem said to Jeroboam: ״Return, and I and you and the son of Jesse will stroll in the Garden of Eden,״ etc., and the mekubbalim said that this stroll is a level of delight beyond which there is none higher — happy is the mortal who merits it. And a person may ask: by what means can a mortal merit this level? For this the statement comes here and says ״if in My statutes״ — through engagement in Torah ״you shall walk״ — this is a going that is hidden from the eyes of all the living, which is the stroll; through the Torah you shall stroll in the hidden places." "It further intends, after the manner of what they said, of blessed memory (Yalkut Tehillim 631), that when HaShem created the first man in this world, He did not create him to dwell in this world forever, even had he not sinned — for if so, it would on the contrary be a descent and lowliness for him, since the ultimate hope is in the supernal world, where he would reap what he sowed. Rather, the intention was that he would ascend to the heavens and to the treasuries of life whenever he wished, and he would be like one who dwells in a house with an upper story above it, and when he wishes to ascend and delight he ascends — as we find (II Kings 2:11) that Elijah ascended in the storm to heaven, and through sin it became necessary to strip his skin from him and leave the body in this world. And Scripture informs us here that through a person's immersion in engagement with Torah, he can attain through it that he will not feel death, but rather like a person walking from one place to another. And you will find (Zohar 2:174a) that the righteous do not feel the pain of separation, after the manner of the verse (Ecclesiastes 5:11), ״מְתוּקָה שְׁנַת הָעוֹבֵד״ — sweet is the sleep of the servant, for one who is a servant of HaShem, his sleep — which is death — is sweet and pleasant to him. And if he intensifies further, behold you have Elijah, who through the intensity of his Torah did not die." "It further intends, after the manner of what they said in Mishnat Chasidim (Avot 4:22): ״against your will you die, and against your will you live״ — that a person does not die by his own choice even if he wished to die. And they said in tractate Niddah (30b), and this is their language: When Rav's soul rested, he said to Rav Assi: ״Go hide it,״ and if he did not obey, ״throw it [גַּרְיֵיהּ].״ He [Rav Assi] thought he said to him ״his kid [גַּדְיֵיהּ].״ After Rav's soul rested, he [Rav] said to him: ״I retract,״ etc. גַּדְיֵיהּ and so forth. The soul of Rav Assi rested. Sheilah went and said to his household: ״Prepare a shield [זְוַודְתָא] for me,״ etc. They prepared a shield for him and the soul of Sheilah rested — end of quotation. Behold that approximately the masters of Torah can go to the supernal world when they wish and desire to go, even though their time has not yet arrived and the King has not sent for them. And this is the meaning of <b>if in My statutes</b> — on account of My statutes in which you toil, you are in your own authority: if you wish to go, you shall go." "Further, HaShem intends to inform us of the surpassing benefit of Torah, for one who engages in Torah will constantly yearn to walk to the supernal world, for he will see the great value of the benefit derived from it and that it is on high, and he will aspire to walk before HaShem in the lands of the living. And along this line I explained the verse's saying (Psalms 49:16) ״אַךְ אֱלֹהִים יִפְדֶּה נַפְשִׁי מִיַּד שְׁאוֹל כִּי יִקָּחֵנִי סֶלָה״ — meaning, when a person is confident that he will not see Sheol and will be restored to the Eden of HaShem, in this he will aspire for סֶלָה to take him, as if it said ״סֶלָה כִּי יִקָּחֵנִי״. And see the Midrash Rabbot (Yalkut Shim'oni, parashat Masei §507) regarding Aaron's statement to Moses when he ascended Mount Hor to die there, that he said to him: had I known this, I would have hoped to go first. And this is the verse's statement, <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> — meaning, when you toil in Torah, through this you yourselves will desire to go and taste a taste beyond compare." "It further intends along the way of their statement in the Book of Zohar (Zohar III, 91a) in explanation of the verse ״שׁוֹר אוֹ כֶשֶׂב״ etc. (Leviticus 22:27): an animal species does not change from the category in which it was born, unlike human beings, who change according to their deeds and ascend from level to supernal level, as they said (Zohar II, 94a) ״זָכָה יַתִּיר יָהֲבִין לֵיהּ״ etc., ״זָכָה יַתִּיר״ etc. — going and growing. And I do not know who it is that encompasses these pathways, and the verse comes here and reveals and says <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> — through engagement in Torah this apprehension will go and shine, from a low rung to a supernal rung and from the supernal to that which is above it." "It may further be explained along the way of their statement, may their memory be for a blessing (Zohar I, 175a), on the verse (Proverbs 6:22) ״בְּהִתְהַלֶּכְךָ תַּנְחֶה אוֹתָךָ״: the pathways along which the soul ascends when it departs from the world are darkness and shadow of death, filled with the wrath of HaShem, and whoever has Torah in his hand — it illuminates before him, as the verse says (Psalms 119:105) ״נֵר לְרַגְלִי דְבָרֶךָ וְאוֹר לִנְתִיבָתִי״. And this is the verse's saying <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b> — meaning, in the Torah you shall walk, for it is a light for your path; but without it, of them it is said (Proverbs 4:19) ״דֶּרֶךְ רְשָׁעִים כָּאֲפֵלָה״ etc. Also, Torah is so called because it is theתּוֹרָה (instruction) of the path upon which one walks." "It may further be explained, apart from their way, may their memory be for a blessing, along this line: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> — the verse speaks of a commandment called חֻקָּה, which is the mitzvah of tefillin, as it says (Exodus 13:10) ״וְשָׁמַרְתָּ אֶת הַחֻקָּה״ etc. And it says ״תֵּלֵכוּ״ along the way of their statement, may their memory be for a blessing: the custom of the early ones was to don tefillin on their arm and head all day long — walking from his house to the study hall with tefillin on his head etc., from the study hall to his house with tefillin etc. And they said (Sukkah 28a) of Rabban Yohanan ben Zakkai and of Rabbi [Yehudah ha-Nasi] that they did not walk four cubits without tefillin, and this is what HaShem says: ״בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ״. And the verse's saying <b>וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b> — these are the commandments pertaining to wearing them, which require a clean body; and according to the one who says (Shabbat 49a) one must not sleep in them; and according to the one who says one must not sleep in them, and one must not divert his mind from them — without this it is forbidden to wear them. And the verse's saying <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם</b> — as if it said ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אַתֶּם״, for through this you prepare yourselves to cause the dwelling of His Shekhinah, may He be blessed, upon you, as the verse says (Deuteronomy 28:10) ״וְרָאוּ כָּל עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ כִּי שֵׁם ה׳ נִקְרָא עָלֶיךָ״ etc., and they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Berakhot 6a) etc." "It may further be explained along the way of their statement (Bereshit Rabbah 44:12) on the verse ״הַבֶּט נָא הַשָּׁמַיְמָה״ — that HaShem lifted Abraham up and set him upon the constellations (mazalot). And this is the verse's saying <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b> — meaning, My חֻקּוֹת (statutes), along the line of the verse (Jeremiah 33:25) ״חֻקּוֹת שָׁמַיִם״ etc.; if upon them you walk, as in (Genesis 13:17) ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ״ — this is a condition: ״וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ״, for through the performance of the mitzvot one rules and treads upon the constellations, and they are that through which Abraham merited this quality. And the verse's saying <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם</b> — ״צֶדֶק יִקְרָאֵהוּ לְרַגְלוֹ״ (Isaiah 41:2), and they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Shabbat 156a), that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him ״מַאי דַּעְתָּךְ דְּקָאֵי צֶדֶק״ etc. — from here, see that one evaluates the constellations in the proper order for himself and moves a constellation from place to place. And this is the verse's saying <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם</b> — meaning, you arrange them for My statutes in the order that is proper for you, and this is their statement, may their memory be for a blessing (Shabbat 156a): ״אֵין מַזָּל לְיִשְׂרָאֵל״ — meaning, they rule over it. Or it may say ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ along the line of the verse (Isaiah 66:22) ״כִּי כַאֲשֶׁר הַשָּׁמַיִם הַחֲדָשִׁים״ etc. ״אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי עוֹשֶׂה״ — it does not say ״עָשִׂיתִי״ but rather ״עוֹשֶׂה״, teaching that through engagement in Torah and mitzvot new heavens are created (Zohar I, 5a). And this is the verse's saying <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם</b>." "It may further be explained along the lines of what they said in tractate Shabbat (156a): one born under the influence of Nogah will be a fornicator. And further there: they said to the mother of Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak that her son would be a thief, etc. — one should look there. We see, then, that the constellation activates within a person the impulse to do evil. And HaShem was concerned lest a person say that since the constellation proclaims that a wicked person born under it has no power to correct himself, he is coerced and exempt from punishment. Therefore He said <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> — these being the constellations — <b>תֵּלֵכוּ</b>, meaning, they will cause you to walk in a path that is not good — you are not exempt by this claim, but rather <b>וְאֶת מְצֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b> — in any case you are obligated, and there is a rectification for the constellation, like the deed that the mother of Rav Nachman did for him, and this is what is said: ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״." "Or it may say along the lines of what they said in tractate Shabbat (156a): this one born under the constellation of Mars will be a shedder of blood. Rav Ashi said: he will be either a blood-letter (umana), or a thief (gannava), or a slaughterer (tavcha), or a mohel (mohala) — and so far [the Gemara resolves the difficulty]. And this is what is meant by <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b> — these being the statutes of the heavens, which are the constellations — <b>וְאֶת מְצֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b>, these being ritual slaughter (shechitah) and circumcision (milah): through this you do what the constellations indicate, in the context of a mitzvah. And this is what is said: <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם</b>." "It may further be explained along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Sifrei, parashat Ha'azinu), that a person must learn moral instruction from the statutes of the heavens, for they do not change their appointed task, and they rejoice and are glad to do the will of the Creator. And this is what is said: <b>אִם</b> — in the manner of My statutes, which are the statutes of heaven and earth that persist continually in doing the will of their Maker with joy and without change — <b>תֵּלֵכוּ</b>: in this manner you too <b>מִצְוֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b> — that you not transgress them, and that you perform what is to be found in action. For you will say: if these, who have neither reward nor punishment, do so — then how much more so must we." "It further intends <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> along the lines of what they said (Avot 3:17): ״אִם אֵין קֶמַח אֵין תּוֹרָה״ etc., which I explained in its place as meaning: ״אִם אֵין קֶמַח״ — if you see that HaShem has shut up the heavens and there is no flour, the reason is ״אֵין תּוֹרָה,״ as it says (Deuteronomy 32:2): ״יַעֲרֹף כַּמָּטָר לִקְחִי״ — meaning, according to the measure of the prepared produce that comes from the rains will be My teaching (לִקְחִי), and when there is no teaching HaShem shuts up the heavens. And their saying ״אִם אֵין תּוֹרָה אֵין קֶמַח״ — meaning, when there is no Torah there will surely be no flour, for HaShem will shut His heavens. And this is what is said: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b>, meaning, by the measure of My statutes, which are the law of sustenance — <b>תֵּלֵכוּ</b>, meaning, if you wish to apportion for yourselves your allotted portion, which is sustenance, you need likewise to fulfill the commandments. And this is what is said: <b>וְאֶת מְצֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b>, along the lines of what is said: ״יַעֲרֹף כַּמָּטָר לִקְחִי״ — according to the measure of the rain, which is sustenance, divine service must be present. And His saying <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם</b> means: [you] shall do them, so that HaShem will sustain the act of giving their allotted portion." "It further intends, along the lines of what is said (Psalms 72:5): ״יִירָאוּךָ עִם שָׁמֶשׁ״, and what they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 29b), that for all created beings the time of their divine service is when the sun rises in the morning — the time of the morning prayer — and when the sun sets, etc. And this is what is said: <b>אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ</b>, along the lines of what is said (Psalms 55:15): ״בְּבֵית אֱלֹהִים נְהַלֵּךְ בְּרָגֶשׁ״. And His saying <b>וְאֶת מְצֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ</b> alludes to what they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 32b): the early pious ones would linger one hour [before prayer], etc., and after prayer one hour, etc.; and corresponding to these He said וְאֶת מְצֺתַי, etc. And His saying <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם</b> means: through this they will rectify the luminaries, for through the deeds of those below, when they walk in a path that is not good, the luminaries are afflicted (Sukkah 29a)." "Further, one may interpret the verse in connection with offerings, for we find that they are called חוקה, as it is written concerning them (Leviticus 6:11): ״חָק עוֹלָם״ etc., and it is written (Leviticus 7:36): ״חֻקַּת עוֹלָם״ etc. And He said <b>if you walk in My statutes</b> — to bring your offerings; this is a condition. <b>And you shall keep My commandments</b> — along the lines of what they said in tractate Sukkah (29a): Rabbi Yohanan said in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yohai: stolen property is analogous to a lame animal, etc. — stolen property too has no remedy either before despair or after despair, etc.; after despair, because of a mitzvah that comes through a transgression. And this is the statement of HaShem here: <b>and you shall keep My commandments</b> — that they should bring neither stolen property before despair nor stolen property after despair. And the masoretic form of the word as one hints to the side that these two commandments are a single matter of theft. And His saying <b>and you shall do them</b> will be explained along the lines of what they said there in the chapter on the lulav, concerning stolen property, and this is their language: Rabbi Yohanan said, what is the meaning of what is written (Isaiah 61:8): ״אֲנִי ה׳ אוֹהֵב מִשְׁפָּט שׂוֹנֵא גָזֵל בְּעוֹלָה״? A parable: a king who passed by the customs house; he said to his servants, ״Pay the tax,״ etc. He said to them: from Me wayfarers should learn not to smuggle past the customs house. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, hates theft in an olah-offering so that they should learn, etc. And they said, their memory for a blessing (Kiddushin 39b), that for one with whom a transgression presents itself and he is saved from it, they give him reward like one who performs a mitzvah. And this is the reason He gave for the mitzvah stated, that they should not bring from stolen property: ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ — meaning, so that they should train themselves as well to flee from theft. And this is called ״doing,״ even though it is nothing but distancing from evil, since it occurs at the time of action, as may be inferred from the expression ״to flee״ etc." "Further, the verse will be explained regarding the totality of commandments in this manner: on the side that a person may think that the essence of the commandments and their benefits applies only to one who knows their essence and reason, but performing a mitzvah without knowing its reason — that is like performing an act with no substance, like a body without a soul. And you will find that this is indeed what they wrote in the name of the Arizal, of blessed memory (Likutei Torah, parashat Ekev): that deed without intention is like an empty vessel, for the essence is the thought. Therefore the statement here comes: <b>if you walk in My statutes</b> — meaning, if a person performs the mitzvah with this intention, toward that which HaShem blessed be He decreed, this intention benefits them as though they fulfilled the commandments as HaShem intended them to be done. And this is His saying <b>and you shall keep My commandments</b> — referring to the Commander, that is, with all their intentions and their secrets. And on the side that a person might say: if so, since the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts this thing on account of the will that is in the person even though he did not intend, then he need not do anything at all and it will be acceptable in his mind to do, and the will will avail even without the deed — Scripture comes to teach: ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ — this is a condition, that he should do what is in his power, and HaShem will complete it on his behalf." "Further, it will be explained along the lines of what Maran ruled in the laws of tefillin, siman 32, se'if 18, and this is his language: if one letter adhered to another, etc., it is invalid; and in the laws of a Torah scroll the Rosh wrote that adhesion invalidates a Torah scroll, and if he scraped away the adhesion, etc., it is valid. We thus find that there is permission to engrave away the adhesive between one letter and another in a Torah scroll but not in tefillin. And this is what Scripture alludes to here: <b>if in My statutes</b> — meaning the engravings of the letters — <b>you walk</b> according to their laws to engrave them; not to erase them entirely, but <b>and My commandments</b> — which are tefillin — <b>you shall keep</b> — not to engrave even the adhesive between one letter and another. And His saying <b>and you shall do them</b> will be explained along the lines of what the Rosh ruled in responsum 16, kelal 3, and this is his language: an open mem whose opening became sealed — one erases the entire letter and writes another in its place; but dragging the closure does not make it a letter, for it would be an engraving from within, and we say (Gittin 20a): ״וְכָתַב״ and not ״חָקַק״ etc., end quote. And this is His saying ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ — meaning, one may not engrave within it but may scrape it and make another in its place." "Further, it alludes according to what the latter authorities raised (Magen Avraham, siman 143): that a Torah scroll in which adhesive is found is permitted to be read from, and there is no need to take out another, for the reason that one can scrape it away, and anything fit for tikkun, etc. (Menachot 103a). And the latter authorities further innovated (Turei Zahav, O.C. siman 32) that even if it is the day of Shabbat and one cannot scrape it, it is permitted, for the reason that the prevention is not on account of the Torah scroll itself but on account of Shabbat observance. And this is His saying <b>if in My statutes</b> — a thing that stands to be engraved, which is the adhesive of a letter; <b>you walk</b> — meaning, you have permission to rule that it is a path they may walk in and will not need to take out another, even if it be at a time when they cannot fix it, which is the holy Shabbat — ״My commandments״ being upon you to prohibit. And this is what He said: ״וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ״. And He gave the reason: <b>and you shall do them</b> — since it is in your power to do, and one can fix it, behold it is valid, for the repair is not a disqualification, according to the reason given by the latter authorities." "Further, it will be explained according to what the Mahariq ruled in Yoreh De'ah, siman 276, se'if 11, and this is his language: if a letter adhered to its fellow in the letters of the Name ב״ה, one may scrape it. And the Mordechai wrote in Menachot, and this is his language: that even though there is a prohibition against erasing the [divine] Name from ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּן כֵּן״ etc., this is nevertheless a tikkun, end quote. This is what He said: <b>if you walk in My statutes</b> — meaning, although I prohibited erasing, if it is a matter that requires engraving away the adhesion, you are permitted to do so. And this is what He said <b>you walk</b> — along the lines of (Exodus 18:20): ״וְהוֹדַעְתָּ לָהֶם אֶת הַדֶּרֶךְ אֲשֶׁר יֵלְכוּ בָהּ״. And His saying <b>and you shall keep My commandments</b> etc. — meaning, this is a condition, to warn his hands that he should touch only the part of the adhesive and not the bodies of the letters concerning which he was commanded not to erase. And He used the plural form to say that HaShem commands concerning the two adhering letters that he should not touch either of them. Perhaps one may also rely on their words, their memory for a blessing, who said (Shevuot 35a) that it is forbidden to erase even the letter attached to the [divine] Name from behind it — and this is what He alluded to by saying <b>My commandments</b> in the plural — the Name and that which is attached to it — that he should keep, so as not to erase by scraping the adhesive. And His saying <b>and you shall do them</b> gives the reason to permit erasing the adhesive according to the words of the Mordechai, that this is nothing but a tikkun. And it also alludes in the statement ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ according to what they said in the beraita (Masekhet Soferim, ch. 5), and this is their language: one who erases one letter, etc. — and if ink spilled over the Name, it is permitted to erase it, for his intention was only to fix. And the Beit Yosef wrote in the name of the R. Y. Iskandarani that even if the letter was invalidated, one may erase the entire letter, end quote. And this is His saying ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ — meaning, even them themselves, which are the letters, if he is compelled to fix, he may erase the letter itself. And what He conditioned — ״וְאֶת מִצְוֹתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ״ not to touch the letters — is when there is only adhesive and the letters are whole; but if the letter was spoiled along with it, he may erase it and fix it." "It may further be explained by way of what they said in the first chapter of Rosh HaShanah (Rosh HaShanah 21a), and this is its language: Rav Huna bar Avin sent to Rava: when you see that the season of Tevet extends until the sixteenth of Nisan, intercalate that year and do not worry, for it is written (Deuteronomy 16:1): ״שָׁמוֹר אֶת חֹדֶשׁ הָאָבִיב״ — keep the spring of the season, that it be in the month of Nisan. End quote. And this is the meaning of His saying <b>if in My statutes you walk</b> — meaning, according to the calculation of intercalation, according to the statutes of the heavens, which are the sun and moon, in knowledge of the time you shall walk; this is a condition — <b>and et My commandments you shall keep</b>, so that the season of spring arrives at Pesach. And if according to the statutes in which you walk it will not be so, <b>and you shall do them</b> — meaning, you shall intercalate the year so that you may perform those commandments, as Rav Huna bar Avin sent to Rava." "It may further be explained by way of what they said in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 156b), and this is its language: Shmuel and Avlat were sitting and those men were going to a marsh, etc. — see, that even though the star had shown concerning him on his journey that he would die, because the man guarded himself from humiliation and did kindness with him, the star's decree was amended for the good. And this is the meaning of His saying <b>if in My statutes you walk</b> — meaning, even though it is a time in which the star has shown concerning him for evil, that he would go and not return — <b>if et My commandments you keep</b>, you have the power to overturn the stars and amend them. And this is the meaning of ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״." "It may further be explained by way of their statement (Pesachim 56a): ״He crushed the bronze serpent״, etc., and they said in the Gemara there, because they went astray after it. Now the matter of the bronze serpent is a statute (חוקה) that HaShem decreed: ״וְהִבִּיט אֶל נְחַשׁ וְגוֹ׳ וָחָי״ (Numbers 21:9), and He permitted them to do so. And this is the meaning of <b>if in My statutes</b> — which is to look upon the bronze serpent — <b>you shall walk</b>; this is a condition — <b>and et My commandments you shall keep</b>. But if not, there is no statute here, and Hezekiah was permitted to crush it. And in this, <b>and you shall do them</b> — so that they not go astray after it." "It may further be explained along this line, that here it speaks of the commandment regarding tevel (untithed produce), for we find that HaShem commanded not to eat tevel, even for a priest who is liable to death. And you may wonder at this: is it truly a matter — for this produce, what forbids it is the separation of terumah, and if terumah itself is eaten by a priest, how much more so a mixture? Rather, this is nothing but a statute (חוקה). And this is the meaning of <b>if in My statutes you walk</b> — not to eat tevel until you perform the commandment of HaShem to separate terumot, etc. And this is the meaning of <b>and et My commandments you shall keep</b>, and then ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ — for through this the separated portion becomes fit to be eaten by its owners, and the remainder by any person." "It further alludes by way of their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Avot 2:10): repent one day before your death — for through this all his days will be in repentance. And this is the meaning of <b>if in My statutes</b> — which is the decree of death that HaShem decreed, from which none escapes — <b>you walk</b> in them, that every day he says: tomorrow I may die. Through this, surely ״וְאֶת מִצְוֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״." "It may further be explained along the lines of what they said in the chapter HaSocher et HaPoalim (Bava Metzia 85a) in that story of the Rabbis, when Elijah told him a sign to recognize the throne of Rabbi Chiya in his ascent from the Garden of Eden below to the heavenly academy — that it rose on its own and did not require angels to lift it as other righteous ones do. This attainment was through the medium of his vigorous exertion in the business of Torah for himself and for others, as stated there. And this is the verse <b>if in My statutes you walk</b> — meaning, through the medium of exertion in Torah, to learn and to teach, as alluded to in the words בחקתי and תלכו — meaning, on your own, like Rabbi Chiya, and you will not need angels to carry you."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {10 "The phrase עַד כָּאן ('end of quotation') marks the close of the cited objection from Sotah.", 11 "The Aramaic phrases from Avodah Zarah 17a are retained as quoted; נִחְלַף ('let him exchange/redirect') introduces each cited opinion.", 12 "The chapter title חֲסִידִים ('pious ones') is the traditional name for the sixth chapter of Avot, commonly called Kinyan HaTorah.", 13 "No translator's note needed.", 17 "The phrase אינה בפרק השג יד is read as 'not beyond reach'; alternatively it could mean 'not within reach' (אינה בהישג יד), but the context — commandments one cannot perform but can study — supports 'beyond reach.' However, the subsequent phrase אותם שאין מציאות לעשותם ('those that have no possibility of being performed') refers to the same category, suggesting the first phrase describes commandments that ARE within reach; the logic then proceeds to those that are not."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:05:41.143203546Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}